oh god  mourners returned to the scene on sunday  to remember the dead and attempt to make  sense of what investigators describe as  a racially motivated hate crime in my 65  years in buffalo i've never seen  anything like this  never we survived the gang era the drug  area and now this we go this too shall  pass we're going to survive this as well  police say the suspect 18-year-old  payton gendron shot 13 people at a  neighborhood grocery store killing ten  most were african-american one aaron  salter was a security guard and a  retired police officer he was a nice guy  you know he cared about the community he  looked after the store investigators say  the suspect had been detained in the  past for threatening remarks but never  criminally charged and that a manifesto  that he appears to have written shows  his actions were racially motivated the  state police had  brought this individual in for a mental  health evaluation  he went to the local hospital he was  there for the information we have is  about a day and a half was evaluated and  then released at that point the buffalo  shooting is just the latest act of  violence in a country struggling with  racial tension gun violence and a surge  in hate crimes prompting calls from  elected officials from the governor all  the way up to the president for change  we must all work together to address the  hate that remains a stain  on the soul of america  the hearts are heavy once again  but a resolve  must never ever waver  new york's governor called for stronger  gun control laws this also calls for a  national response what was used  was not able to be purchased legally in  the state of new york the basic gun was  but the high capacity magazine  associated with it had to come from  another state because it is illegal in  the state of new york we need a smart  national policy she said the  investigation would also focus on what  more could have been done to stop him  kristen salumi al jazeera new york

